  "item": "impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[read]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This required SourceProvider callback is an error guard: the fixture\n supplies cached snapshots by overriding parse. Structural search must\n use those snapshots without refetching. Calling read would fail the\n contract tested below; primitive reads are exercised by other providers."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` is a valid coverage exception.\n\n- **Form:** It is a fenced `{cite}` with commentary on an explicit function with a body, which is the form the built-in allows.\n- **Reason is substantive:** The commentary says `read` is a required trait callback that works as an error guard. `CachedParse` overrides `parse` to supply cached snapshots, and structural search must not refetch. The fixture's `read` returns `SourceError::Invalid(\"cached parser must not refetch\")`, so a call would fail the contract. That is a real reason the function should not run, not just difficulty in covering it.\n- **Agrees with the source:**\n  - `fn[read]` does return that error.\n  - `parse` is overridden to build the snapshot with `parse_snapshot` and to count its calls.\n  - `fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` runs `search_structure` three times and unwraps each result. It asserts that `parses` equals 3 and that matches come from the cached snapshot. A call to `read` would return an error, so the test would fail.\n  - Other tests, such as `fn[defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot]`, `fn[local_provider_scopes_paths_and_rejects_resources_outside_its_root]` and `fn[successful_empty_listing_and_empty_resources_are_distinct_from_errors]`, call `read` on other providers. This supports the claim that primitive reads are exercised elsewhere.\n- **Not redundant:** The item carries no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference that would make the citation redundant.",
